A point on the line is given by
\[\begin{pmatrix} x \\ y \\ z \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 4 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} + t \begin{pmatrix} -2 \\ 6 \\ -3 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 4 - 2t \\ 6t \\ 1 - 3t \end{pmatrix}.\][asy]
unitsize (0.6 cm);

pair A, B, C, D, E, F, H;

A = (2,5);
B = (0,0);
C = (8,0);
D = (A + reflect(B,C)*(A))/2;

draw(A--D);
draw((0,0)--(8,0));

dot("$(2,3,4)$", A, N);
dot("$(4 - 2t, 6t, 1 - 3t)$", D, S);
[/asy]

The vector pointing from $(2,3,4)$ to $(4 - 2t, 6t, 1 - 3t)$ is then
\[\begin{pmatrix} 2 - 2t \\ -3 + 6t \\ -3 - 3t \end{pmatrix}.\]For the point on the line that is closest to $(2,3,4),$ this vector will be orthogonal to the direction vector of the second line, which is $\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\ 6 \\ -3 \end{pmatrix}.$  Thus,
\[\begin{pmatrix} 2 - 2t \\ -3 + 6t \\ -3 - 3t \end{pmatrix} \cdot \begin{pmatrix} -2 \\ 6 \\ -3 \end{pmatrix} = 0.\]This gives us $(2 - 2t)(-2) + (-3 + 6t)(6) + (-3 - 3t)(-3) = 0.$  Solving, we find $t = \frac{13}{49}.$

For this value of $t,$ the point is $\boxed{\left( \frac{170}{49}, \frac{78}{49}, \frac{10}{49} \right)}.$

response:
 Let $y = \frac{x^2}{x - 8}.$  Then
\[y(x - 8) = x^2.\]Hence,
\[(y - 8)x^2 + zx - 8y = 0.\]For a given $y,$ this quadratic has a real root in $x$ in either of two ways: The quadratic has a double root, or it has two distinct real roots.  If the polynomial has nonnegative discriminant, then it will have real roots, and we'll get a value for $x$ at which the given function is defined.  If the polynomial has a double root, then that root will correspond to a place at which the given function achieves its minimum value $y.$

The discriminant of the quadratic is
\begin{align*}
z^2 & = 4 \cdot 8 \cdot y \cdot (y - 8) &= 32 y^2 - 256 y \\
\\
&= 32y (y - 8).
\end{align*}The quadratic has a double root if and only if $z = 0,$ in which case
\[y (y - 8) = 0.\]We can eliminate numbers larger than 8 (since $x > 8,$ which means $x - 8 > 0,$ and so $y > 0,$ and so $y - 8 > -8,$ which means $y < 8,$ and so $y (y - 8) < 0.$) The minimum value of $y$ in our case is therefore $ Berger2 | 8.$

It is still possible that the quadratic does not have real roots for the value of $y,$ in which case it does not attain a minimum value. Now, when it does have real roots, it does not matter how we define the minimum value, as we just found it as the value $y$ for which we had a double root.

